{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>If you will surely listen to the voice of YHWH your Elohim</b> — Ibn Ezra (on Exodus 15:26) explained [this] to mean understanding the reason for what He commands you to do. <b>And that which is upright in His eyes you shall do</b> — positive commandments. <b>And you shall give ear to His commandments</b> — negative commandments. And in the Mekhilta (here) they said: <b>and that which is upright in His eyes you shall do</b> — this refers to business dealings, teaching that anyone who conducts commerce in faithfulness and whose spirit is well-disposed toward people, Scripture accounts it as though he fulfilled the entire Torah in its entirety. And I will further explain this when I reach the verse \"and you shall do what is upright and good\" (Deuteronomy 6:18), if the good El will deal kindly with me:" "<b>All the disease which I put upon Egypt I will not put upon you</b> — in the language of Rashi (on Exodus 15:26): I will not put upon you, and if I do put [them upon you], they are as though they were never put, for I am YHWH your healer. This is its midrash. And its plain sense: like a person who says, I am the physician who warns you not to eat things that cause a person to return to his illness — thus far [Rashi's words]. But the plain sense of the verse is that <b>your healer</b> is an epithet, and it is not the way of a master to guarantee his servants that if you do all my will and desire I will not kill you with terrible diseases — and the same holds for all the guarantees in the Torah. Rather, this is a warning: He warns them not to be among those who rebel against Him like the Egyptians, for when they hear His voice they will be saved from all that disease, since all that disease is fitting to come upon all who transgress His will, just as it came upon the Egyptians who did not listen to Him. And this is in the manner of what He said He would put upon you: \"all the diseases of Egypt which you dreaded, and they shall cling to you\" (Deuteronomy 28:60). And He said <b>for I am YHWH your healer</b> — a guarantee that He will remove disease from your midst, coming in the manner of all the earth, just as He healed the waters. Ibn Ezra (on Exodus 15:26) said that this sign, which is the first in the wilderness, is the reverse of the first plague in Egypt: the waters of the Nile were sweet and He turned them to evil, whereas these [waters] were bitter and He healed them. Thus HaShem does a thing and its opposite; therefore you should fear Him lest you rebel against Him and He not heal you as He did them, and love Him, for He will do you good just as He healed the waters for you. And the subject of the verse speaks in two respects: His commandments and His statutes, and \"I will not put upon you, for I am YHWH\" — I have already written to you concerning this on the thirtieth [commentary, referring to Exodus 15:2] in explanation of its meaning. It will be understood from the word <b>voice</b> (לְקוֹל) and from the word <b>I</b> (אֲנִי): if we listen to the voice of our Elohim to keep the commandments of YHWH and His statutes, the glorious HaShem will be our healer. From here they instituted in the blessings \"who has sanctified us with His commandments and commanded us,\" for the blessing contains [an acknowledgment of] sovereignty, and the commandments were commanded to us from eternity to eternity. והמשכיל יבין. And I will enlighten your eyes: every blessing that contains [an acknowledgment of] sovereignty follows this pattern, for they ascribed honor to the sovereignty of the Universe — \"who has sanctified us\" and \"who has made for us\" — whereas those adjacent to them in which they do not mention sovereignty, they address directly: \"You are mighty\" (אַתָּה גִּבּוֹר), \"You are holy\" (אַתָּה קָדוֹשׁ), and so all of them. And in \"Aleinu leshabe'ah\" they concealed it because they mention in it \"before the King, King of kings.\" And understand this."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {1 "Ramban's cross-reference to 'the thirtieth' appears to refer back to his earlier commentary on Exodus 15:2; the exact referent is uncertain but the sense is clear. The phrase 'והמשכיל יבין' (the enlightened will understand) is left untranslated per voice rules governing kabbalistic hints."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:16:31.761054133Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}